# Container images

The release workflow publishes two alternative multi-arch Linux images to GitHub
Container Registry (GHCR):

| Engine | Image | API and UI |
| --- | --- | --- |
| LangGraph | `ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-lang` | API at `/v1`, console at `/ui` |
| Google ADK | `ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-adk` | API at `/v1`, ADK UI at `/dev-ui/` and fallback console at `/ui` |

Choose one engine for a deployment. Both expose port `8080` in the container
and use the same health and OpenAI-compatible API surface.

## Pulling images

Packages are private by default, so authenticate before pulling unless a
maintainer has explicitly made the package public:

```bash
docker pull ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-lang:0.1.0
docker pull ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-adk:0.1.0
```

Replace `0.1.0` with a published full release version (without the Git tag's
leading `v`). Full version tags are immutable. For a private package, use a
classic personal access token with the `read:packages` scope:

```bash
echo "$GHCR_TOKEN" | docker login ghcr.io -u YOUR_GITHUB_USERNAME --password-stdin
```

Images are published for `linux/amd64` and `linux/arm64`. Docker selects the
matching architecture automatically; on a host that does not match either,
explicitly opt into emulation with `--platform linux/amd64` or
`--platform linux/arm64`. The release workflow uses a registry-backed
`buildcache` tag for layer caching; that tag is not a deployment target.

## Run an image

The image includes the example application, so this starts LangGraph on host
port 8081:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8081:8080 \
  -e OPENAI_API_KEY \
  ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-lang:0.1.0
```

Use `http://localhost:8081/v1` for the API and
`http://localhost:8081/ui` for the LangGraph console. The equivalent ADK
command is:

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8082:8080 \
  -e GOOGLE_API_KEY \
  ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-adk:0.1.0
```

Its API is `http://localhost:8082/v1`; its native UI is
`http://localhost:8082/dev-ui/` (with `/ui` available as a fallback). Set
`SPECS_AGENT_API_KEY` to require a bearer token or `X-API-Key` on protected
API endpoints. Do not pass provider credentials in an image, Dockerfile, or
version-controlled environment file.

Both images expose interactive API documentation at `/docs`, ReDoc at `/redoc`,
and the runtime OpenAPI document at `/openapi.json`. Set
`SHUTDOWN_GRACE_PERIOD_SECONDS` to control in-flight request draining when the
container receives a termination signal; the default is 30 seconds.

### Mount specifications and persistent data

Mount an application bundle read-only and override the image command with its
path. A named volume retains SQLite checkpoints at `/data/checkpoints.db`:

```bash
docker volume create specs-lang-data
docker run --rm -p 8081:8080 \
  -v "$PWD/examples/application:/specs:ro" \
  -v specs-lang-data:/data \
  -e CHECKPOINT_DB_PATH=/data/checkpoints.db \
  -e OPENAI_API_KEY \
  ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-lang:0.1.0 \
  /app/.venv/bin/open-agent-lang serve --application /specs --host 0.0.0.0
```

For ADK, substitute the image and executable with
`specs-agent-adk` and `/app/.venv/bin/open-agent-adk`, and supply
`GOOGLE_API_KEY` as appropriate. Keep LangGraph and ADK checkpoint volumes
separate. You may instead mount individual files and set
`WORKFLOW_SPEC_PATH` and `AGENT_SPEC_PATH`.

For local source builds and Compose profiles, see the Docker Compose section
in the [README](../README.md#docker-compose-deployment). Compose uses distinct
`lang_data` and `adk_data` volumes and local image names;
`specs-agent-lang:local` and `specs-agent-adk:local` are not GHCR packages.

## Tags and visibility

Each image receives an immutable `sha-<full-commit-SHA>` traceability tag and,
for a release, its immutable full semantic version (for example, `0.1.0`).
Builds also carry standard OCI labels for source, revision, and version.
Stable releases also update the
`0`, `0.1`, and `latest` aliases; prereleases do not update `latest`.
Deploy production workloads by full version or digest, not a moving alias.

New GHCR packages default to private. Repository linkage can inherit repository
*access permissions*, but it does not make a package public or inherit
visibility. Keep packages private unless public consumption is explicitly
approved. Changing a package to public is irreversible: anyone can pull its
current and future public versions. If made public, retain stable full-version
tags and their OCI attestations; clean up short-lived prerelease tags only
under a documented retention policy.
`edge` is not currently published; do not introduce or retain an `edge` tag
until an approved main-branch policy exists. Do not delete a digest still
referenced by a release, deployment, SBOM, or attestation.

## Supply-chain artifacts

Release images are built as untagged OCI digests with BuildKit provenance
(`mode=max`) and SBOM attestations stored alongside the GHCR image. Each remote
digest is then scanned with Trivy before release tags are added. The workflow
uploads SARIF vulnerability results and a review SBOM artifact. Pull requests
build and scan both images but never receive package-write permission and never
publish.
The Trivy severity gate is always enforced for fixable HIGH and CRITICAL
findings. Unfixed findings remain visible in the generated report so they can be
tracked without making every release permanently impossible. SARIF upload is
best-effort because GitHub code scanning for a private repository requires
GitHub Advanced Security; the SARIF and SBOM files remain workflow artifacts
when that upload is not available.

Inspect the OCI attestations stored with a digest:

```bash
docker buildx imagetools inspect \
  ghcr.io/bassemzohdy/specs-agent-lang@sha256:REPLACE_WITH_DIGEST
```

Use the digest shown by `docker buildx imagetools inspect` or the package page,
and inspect its attestations with a registry-aware OCI tool as needed; repeat
the command with `specs-agent-adk` for the ADK image. GitHub artifact
attestations remain deferred because they are unsupported for this private
personal repository. See
[release operations](releasing.md) for the publishing checklist.
